### .NET version

.NET 10.0.12 (net10.0-windows), the code is the same in main

### Did it work in .NET Framework?

Yes

### Did it work in any of the earlier releases of .NET Core or .NET 5+?

Yes, .NET 9 (9.0.20) and .NET 8 keep a current cell

### Issue description

When a row is removed from a `DataGridView`, the grid no longer moves the current cell to a neighbouring row. After the user deletes the current row with the Delete key, `CurrentCell` and `CurrentRow` are `null` and no row is selected. Pressing Delete again or typing does nothing until the user clicks a row. `Rows.RemoveAt` on an unbound grid behaves the same.

The cause is a change in `DataGridViewRowCollection.RemoveAtInternal` by #12534 (Finish enabling unnecessary assignment analyzer):

```diff
-        Point newCurrentCell = new(-1, -1);
         ...
-        DataGridView.OnRemovingRow(index, out newCurrentCell, force);
+        DataGridView.OnRemovingRow(index, out _, force);
         ...
-        OnCollectionChanged(new CollectionChangeEventArgs(CollectionChangeAction.Remove, dataGridViewRow), index, 1, true, false, false, newCurrentCell);
+        OnCollectionChanged(new CollectionChangeEventArgs(CollectionChangeAction.Remove, dataGridViewRow), index, 1, true, false, false, new Point(-1, -1));
```

`OnRemovingRow` clears the current cell and returns the new one (the next visible row, or the previous one), and `OnRemovedRow_PostNotification` used to set it. Since the returned value is discarded, the current cell stays cleared. The analyzer only flagged the unused initializer `new(-1, -1)`; keeping `out newCurrentCell` and passing it on restores the behaviour.

Removing an item from a bound `BindingList` outside a user deletion still ends with a current cell, because the grid matches the `CurrencyManager` position afterwards.

Workaround for user deletions: a derived grid remembers `CurrentCellAddress` in `OnUserDeletingRow` and sets `CurrentCell` again in `OnUserDeletedRow`.

### Steps to reproduce

```xml
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFrameworks>net9.0-windows;net10.0-windows</TargetFrameworks>
    <UseWindowsForms>true</UseWindowsForms>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
```

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;

internal static class Program {
    [STAThread]
    private static void Main() {
        Console.WriteLine($".NET {Environment.Version}");
        Check("bound, user presses Delete", grid => grid.PressDelete());
        Check("bound, item removed from the list", grid => ((BindingList<Item>)grid.DataSource!).RemoveAt(1));
        Check("unbound, Rows.RemoveAt", grid => grid.Rows.RemoveAt(1), bound: false);
    }

    private static void Check(string what, Action<TestGrid> delete, bool bound = true) {
        using Form form = new();
        TestGrid grid = new() { Dock = DockStyle.Fill };
        form.Controls.Add(grid);
        if (bound) {
            grid.DataSource = new BindingList<Item> { new("A"), new("B"), new("C") };
        } else {
            grid.Columns.Add("Name", "Name");
            grid.Rows.Add("A");
            grid.Rows.Add("B");
            grid.Rows.Add("C");
        }
        form.Show();
        grid.CurrentCell = grid.Rows[1].Cells[0];

        delete(grid);

        string current = grid.CurrentCell is null ? "null" : $"row {grid.CurrentCell.RowIndex}";
        Console.WriteLine($"{what,-36} CurrentCell: {current,-6} SelectedRows: {grid.SelectedRows.Count}");
    }
}

internal sealed class Item(string name) {
    public Item() : this("") { }
    public string Name { get; set; } = name;
}

internal sealed class TestGrid : DataGridView {
    public TestGrid() {
        SelectionMode = DataGridViewSelectionMode.FullRowSelect;
    }

    public void PressDelete() => ProcessDeleteKey(Keys.Delete);
}
```

`dotnet run -f net9.0-windows` and `dotnet run -f net10.0-windows`:

```
.NET 9.0.20
bound, user presses Delete           CurrentCell: row 1  SelectedRows: 1
bound, item removed from the list    CurrentCell: row 1  SelectedRows: 1
unbound, Rows.RemoveAt               CurrentCell: row 1  SelectedRows: 1
.NET 10.0.12
bound, user presses Delete           CurrentCell: null   SelectedRows: 0
bound, item removed from the list    CurrentCell: row 1  SelectedRows: 1
unbound, Rows.RemoveAt               CurrentCell: null   SelectedRows: 0
```

Expected: the row after the removed one is current and selected, as in .NET 9.

