### Issue description

We have dynamically generated context menus, based on the state of our application. The way we implemented it is by overriding the ContextMenuStrip property of some controls, having it regenerate the contents of the menu. We cannot rely on the Opening event because we need the context menu to be rebuilt also when resolving keyboard shortcuts.

Enter ToolStripManager.ProcessShortcut that iterates on the list of all ToolStrips in the thread, evaluating `toolStrip == activeControl.ContextMenuStrip`. This will not only cause performance issues (regenerating a single menu as many times as there are instances of toolstrip), but the call to ContextMenuStrip, calling our menu generation function can cause the ToolStrips list to be modified, resulting in a crash because a list was modified while iterated over.


### Steps to reproduce

Here is an example of a control class with a dynamic context menu:
```
        class C : Control
        {
            public C()
            {
                ContextMenuStrip = ctx;
            }
            ContextMenuStrip ctx = new();
            public override ContextMenuStrip? ContextMenuStrip
            {
                get
                {
                    var l = new List<ToolStripItem> ();
                    foreach (var o in ctx.Items) l.Add(o as ToolStripItem);
                    ctx.Items.Clear();
                    foreach (var it in l)
                    {
                        it.Dispose(); // a ToolStripMenuItem with a sub menu will be removed from ToolStripManager.ToolStrips
                    }
                    ToolStripMenuItem it1 = new ToolStripMenuItem() { Text = "aaa" };
                    ToolStripMenuItem it2 = new ToolStripMenuItem() { Text = "bb" };
                    it1.DropDownItems.Add(it2);
                    ctx.Items.Add(it1);
                    return ctx;
                }
                set => base.ContextMenuStrip = value;
            }
        }
```

Showing it in a form as in

```
        void DynamicMenuCrash()
        {
            using var f = new Form();
            f.Controls.Add(new C() { Dock = DockStyle.Fill });
            f.Show();
            Application.Run(f);
        }
```

and pressing some shortcut key (e.g. F5) will raise this exception:


